# Manuscript PDF page 83

                                 MIKEY



flipped the elastic back, and wrote one line, slowly, in a small neat
hand.
    “What did you write?”
    “A note to myself. I forget kindnesses if I don’t record them.” He
closed the book. “You tap when it’s too quiet, don’t you?”
    The stairwell was very still.
    “We built the early program for children like you,” he said, in the
voice of a man being honest. “Children who hear more than they
should. So they wouldn’t have to carry it. We take it off them, a little at
a time. I know how that sounds. But I count, Juan Carlos. I’m one of the
few people who does. Do you know how many of the children in that
pilot are alive and employed today? I do.”
    He waited. One. Two. Three.
    “You counted me,” I said.
    “Level two.” His smile was almost fond. “A hopeful number.”
    Five. Six. Seven. I felt it come down on me like a hand on the back of
my neck.
    “Thursday,” he said, “is our first listening session. At the Ballroom,
your own building. Come. Hear what we’re making. Bring your friend
with the drum. Manuel, isn’t it?” Eight. “Mikey would be glad to see
you.”
    He stepped around me and went up the last flight, taking his time,
to Doña Luz’s door. He knocked twice, politely. Nothing moved behind
it. He bent, set the basket on the mat above BENDICIONES, and
straightened.
    “Neighbor,” he said, to the closed door.
    Then he went back down past me, and nodded, and was gone, and I
heard the lobby door close, and a car I couldn’t see pull away from the
curb with no hurry at all.
    The door of 4C stayed shut. The basket stayed on the mat. Inside, a
chain slid across with a very small, final click.



I went to my apartment. I sat on my bed with my phone.




